Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero self-hosting compiler implementation

compiler.zero is the complete canonical compiler source, written in Zero.
Normal builds compile that file directly. The selfhost/ fragments are a
readable development layout, not a complete current concatenation recipe.
compiler-head.zero.part and compiler-core.zero.part are exact excerpts of the
current canonical header and decoder/parser/finalizer/output region;
finalizer.zero.part is also an exact excerpt. Template parts document their
respective instruction banks and historical extensions.

Current portability source also lives outside this directory:
  portability/host/linux-tables.zero.part
  portability/host/windows-tables.zero.part
  portability/host/windows-finalizer.zero.part
  portability/host/fallback-tables.zero.part
  portability/windows/x64-windows.zero.part
  portability/windows/finalize-windows.zero.part
  portability/guest/guest-tables.zero.part
  portability/guest/biosmove/real-biosmove.zero.part
The associated apply.py scripts record reviewed development integration steps.
They are not rerun against the already merged compiler by a normal build.
No native compiler binary is embedded; instruction tables encode original
primitives and executable/firmware/application ABIs.

Historical one-time 4 MiB bootstrap transition:
  bootstrap/zero-0.6-seed --host < bootstrap/grow-host-4m.zero > grow-host-4m
  bootstrap/zero-0.6-seed --host < compiler.zero > stage1-old
  grow-host-4m < stage1-old > stage1
  stage1 --host < compiler.zero > stage2
  stage2 --host < compiler.zero > stage3
The existing own compiler seed is used only to cross the historical bootstrap
boundary. The patcher itself is Zero and verifies our complete 41-byte hosted
prologue before changing its arena-size immediate at file offset 151. Subsequent
generations emit their own 4 MiB hosted arena prologue and need no patcher.
The patched first generation and generations 2/3 are byte-identical.

Compiler flags: no flag or --host -> ELF64; --raw/--efi -> x64;
--raw32/--efi32 -> IA32; --real ->386+ real-mode boot image;
--windows -> Windows x64 console PE; --app64/--app32 -> ZEROAPP1 native apps.
Default ELF and --host have a 4 MiB arena; --host additionally permits stores.
The initial Linux process stack is inspected only through our generated own
entry ABI: arena_base + 4194304 + 512 holds argc, +528 holds argv[1].

Memory layout, offsets relative to a single 4,022,880-byte reservation:
       0  source buffer, 262,145 bytes (one overflow-detection byte)
  262160  262,145 tokens, 12 bytes each (u32 kind and u64 payload)
 3407904  generated output, 491,520 bytes
 3899440  action/continuation stack, 32,768 bytes
 3932224  symbol records, 2,048 bytes
 3934272  three 128-entry template banks, 6,144 bytes
 3940416  decoded template storage, 32,768 bytes
 3973184  reserved string scratch, 4,096 bytes
 3977880  builtin metadata (73 slots of 24 bytes, including retired-name holes)
There are 59 Zero variables. The worst-case 262,145 token records fit;
the EOF record is explicit. Source bytes are never changed after tokenization.

Parsing uses explicit 24-byte action frames, so the compiler needs neither
native recursion nor user-level functions. Actions implement one comparison,
left-associative addition/subtraction/multiplication, recursive unary/primary
expressions, argument lists, bindings, conditionals and loops. The active unary
frame limit is 64, matching the previous parser's parentheses/unary budget.
Block nesting is limited to 64. All declarations are top-level and there are
at most 64 symbols. All variable lookup is exact, case-sensitive ASCII.

Every source byte is read before parsing. Oversized input and unsupported
operations fail with status 1. No output is written until tokenization,
parsing, target code emission and executable-header finalization succeed.
Input/output retry EINTR; final output handles short writes. Output is capped
at 491,520 bytes, or 16,384 bytes for the real target. A later write error can
necessarily leave a partial stream, but compile errors never do.

Current lexical policy reserves all supported builtin names across all targets.
This is a unified-language namespace, unlike the old separate executables'
target-dependent reservations. Unsupported target operations still reject.
Diagnostics in this initial compiler use nonzero exit status without stderr.

Action IDs:
 1 statements, 2 statement, 3 binding, 4 effect terminator
 10 expression, 11 comparison tail, 12 comparison emission
 14 additive, 15 additive tail, 16 binary emission
 17 product, 18 product tail, 20 unary/primary, 21 negation
 23 string payload, 24 close parenthesis, 25 leave unary frame
 31 builtin, 32 finish builtin, 33 arguments, 34 far transition
 40 conditional body, 41 conditional/loop completion, 42 else completion
 90 emit target template, 99 complete program

Cooperative native threads
--------------------------
:fork[context | stackBase | stackBytes] creates a suspended native continuation.
The parent receives 0. When !switch[fromContext | toContext] activates the new
continuation, the same expression receives 1 in the child. These operations
exist for Linux/Windows hosted x64, raw/EFI x64 and raw/EFI IA32; real and
ZEROAPP1 targets reject them.
fork and switch evaluate and push every argument in source order. Their native
templates consume all argument words before measuring the live operand stack.

The context is 80 bytes, aligned to 8. Its fields are 64-bit slots; IA32 stores
zero in each high word:
   0 continuation program counter
   8 native stack pointer
  16 native frame pointer
  24 arena allocation cursor
  32 boot argument0
  40 boot argument1
  48 native flags
  56 resume expression result (new child 1; suspended switch 0)
  64 arena base / baseline native stack pointer
  72 context version 1, published only after successful initialization

stackBase must be 16-byte aligned and stackBytes must be at least 81920. The
runtime validates address arithmetic, actual room for live operands, and
relevant overlaps; failures detected by these guards trap with UD2. Pointers
must refer to caller-owned valid memory. Context records
must be separate from the destination child stack. !switch requires different
source and destination context records and a valid initialized destination.

Child BP = align_down(stackBase + stackBytes,16) - 16.
Child arena base = child BP - 512 - 65536.
Child SP = child arena base - live operand bytes.
The 512-byte local frame and all live expression operand words are copied.
The child receives a fresh private 64 KiB reserve arena. Existing pointer values
are preserved exactly and still refer to shared memory. No untyped integer is
guessed to be a pointer or implicitly rebased. Allocating a child stack inside
the parent's reserve arena is therefore valid for hosted tests.

Raw x64 tracks baseline in R12, cursor in R13, boot arguments in R14/R15.
Current hosted R14 instead retains the original argument frame; Windows R15
is the PE image base. Cooperative contexts preserve these values unchanged.
R12 initialization is appended to the existing prologue without moving SP/BP,
so the Linux argc/argv bootstrap offsets do not change. The foreign call
template saves/restores R12 around its existing stack-alignment scratch use.
IA32 uses its existing 16-byte frame header: arg0+0, arg1+4, cursor+8, baseline+12.

This is cooperative scheduling in the shared kernel address space, with real
independent native stacks and program counters. It provides no preemption,
memory protection, SMP synchronization or interruption inside firmware calls.
All scheduler decisions and task bookkeeping are implemented in Zero kernel
source. No functions, assembly source, external compiler or linker is required.

Additional real-mode graphics primitives:
  :vbe[ax | bx | cx | buffer] invokes the selected VBE BIOS service and returns AX.
  :far16[segment | offset] reads a word through a bounded real-mode segment.
VBE pushes all four arguments; far16 uses the standard binary ECX/EAX ABI.

The threading revision was bootstrapped from preserved own version 0.5 seed
bootstrap/zero-0.5-seed (SHA256 419f29a1c509f3ede1b092ed9bc716cc62169e4b8ef1647028652a6403e64a91).
The first generation still had the old entry prologue; generations 2 and 3 are
byte-identical; that historical fixed point is preserved as the 0.6 seed.
That version 0.6 compiler source was 52,656 bytes and its executable 58,959 bytes.
Historical SHA256: 3f9da290f0afc54da61c02297ca9a385b70a49da45fc5094688f966904ad98c6.
Independent validation includes hosted native two-worker execution, x64 raw
firmware-call preservation followed by threads, IA32 raw threading without
long mode or PAE, IA32 full-u64 regression, EFI32/raw text parity, malformed
primitive arity/target checks, and eight runtime-guard checks on each native
architecture. Reproducible fixtures and evidence are under review-tests/threads
and verification/threads. No assembler or linker was used.

Filesystem-development primitives
---------------------------------
Linux hosted x64 only:
  :pread[fd | buffer | count | offset] reads at an explicit 64-bit file offset.
  :pwrite[fd | buffer | count | offset] writes at an explicit 64-bit file offset.
  :fsync[fd] flushes the open descriptor.
The first two evaluate and push all four arguments. Their templates pop offset
into R10, count into RDX, buffer into RSI and descriptor into RDI, then issue Linux
syscall 17/18. fsync moves its unary value into RDI and issues syscall 74. Results
are the direct unsigned 64-bit representation of Linux return values, including
negative errno. Callers handle EINTR, short transfers and errors. Positioned I/O
does not alter the descriptor's current offset. No file-opening API is added;
the validation shell supplies an already-open descriptor, normally 3.

All targets support :bitxor[left | right], :bitand[left | right] and
:shr[value | count]. The first two operate on all value bits; shr is logical
unsigned right shift. Counts>=64 return 0 on x64/IA32; counts>=32 return 0 on the
u32 real target. IA32 implements full 64-bit value and count semantics; a nonzero
high count word therefore returns 0. Operands follow the usual binary ABI.

Real-only :memmap[continuation | buffer] queries BIOS INT15/E820. The buffer must
be a physical address<=0xffe0 with 28 writable bytes. The wrapper requests 24-byte
SMAP records, initializes the extended attribute word at +20 to 1, and records
returned ECX at +24. It returns the next EBX continuation (0 means end), or
0xffffffff for an invalid buffer, carry, wrong SMAP signature, or returned
record size outside 20..24. Invalid buffer is rejected before dereferencing it.
The wrapper preserves general registers except its EAX result, DS/ES and flags,
and clears DF after returning. Kernel stack placement is a separate Zero BIOS
loader policy using the returned usable-memory records.

The previous 0.6 compiler/source are preserved in bootstrap/zero-0.6-seed,
bootstrap/compiler-0.6.zero and bootstrap/zero-0.6.ztext. The 0.7 baseline
source/executable are also preserved as bootstrap/compiler-0.7.zero and
zero-0.7-seed. Current 0.10 source is 86992 bytes; executable 91573 bytes, SHA256:
911f6d67361300755c13bbf5f3d231d54ceae5736f1ec5e60dde5d6e4095ed3a.
The preceding 0.9 source/seed are preserved under bootstrap/*0.9*.
Compiler capacities grow; the language still permits 64 local variable slots,
64 nested expression frames and 64 nested blocks. Raw/EFI and child reserve
arenas remain 64 KiB. Real output remains capped at 16 KiB.

Dword I/O extension: builtin metadata 54/55 map to template 82/83; token
range now 500..572, with existing retired-name holes unchanged. Target banks
include x64-port32.zero.part, ia32-port32.zero.part and real-port32.zero.part.
The parser uses existing binary operand-pop handling for out32; in32 is unary.
See port32.txt and verification/network-ports for reviewed encodings and tests.

Merged 0.9 target extensions
Windows mode 7 overrides hosted templates and emits original PE/import metadata.
It supports binary KERNEL32 input/output and original cooperative contexts,
but rejects firmware/ports and Unix positioned I/O. Current 0.10 also permits
explicit Microsoft-ABI native call on --host and --windows. Guest modes 8/9 override
entry/arena/stream/return templates and add ZEROAPP1 header 84. They reject
firmware/ports, foreign calls, fork/switch and Unix file descriptors. Their
application context and memory ownership are in portability/guest/ABI.ztext.
The real-only biosmove builtin 56/token 556 uses template 85; its descriptor
policy stays in the loader. That 0.9 revision reserved 3998304 bytes for capacity 491520 inside the 4 MiB
hosted arena. The current 0.10 memory table above supersedes that layout.
Final merged checks are under verification/compiler-0.9; earlier extension
reports remain separate evidence tied to their own recorded compiler hashes.

Current 0.10 host extension
Builtin indices 57..72, tokens 557..572 and templates 86..101 are the 16 operations
specified in portability/host/ABI.ztext. They retain strict value/effect forms,
arity validation and a globally reserved namespace. Host templates override
the active Linux/Windows bank; guest templates return explicit unavailable
sentinels. Raw/EFI/real expose only host[]=0 from this extension.
Binary operands use the existing pop preparation. Ternary file I/O and memory
protection push all three values; templates consume their complete operand
stack. Windows and Linux preserve RBP/RSP and R12..R15 as required by Zero.

Decoded table storage grows from 8192 to 32768; scratch and metadata move 24576 bytes,
with total reservation 4022880. Source, tokens, output 491520, parser stack and
symbol/template banks retain their offsets. The Linux hosted prologue adds
MOV R14,RBP to preserve the original argument frame across fork/switch. Its
44-byte size explains the historical seed transition; the current seed reaches
its own fixed point without a binary patcher. Raw/EFI/guest entry bytes do not
change for ordinary programs that do not use the new operations.

Optional fragments remain readable development excerpts, not a complete
concatenation recipe. Normal builds compile canonical compiler.zero directly.
Current evidence: verification/host-api and portability/host/*verification.json.
